\section{Data representation}{

An \code{\link{AnvlArray}} with \code{backend = "r"} is, under the hood, a plain R vector or
array (\code{numeric}, \code{integer}, or \code{logical}) stored in the \verb{$data} field.
\code{\link[=as_array]{as_array()}} returns the underlying vector/array directly without copying,
and \code{\link[=nv_array]{nv_array()}} simply wraps an R vector/array. The only device is
\code{\link[=r_device]{r_device("cpu")}}.
}

\section{Status}{

This backend is \strong{experimental}. Only a subset of the primitives that the
PJRT backend supports are currently lowered to R code. See
the \href{https://r-xla.github.io/anvl/articles/primitives.html}{Primitives Reference} article for an overview (the column of the quickr
backend).
}

\section{Supported data types}{

\code{f64}, \code{i32} and \code{bool} -- the three R storage types.
